2026-08-31T06:00Z claude note the author ruled 2026-08-31 that the embedder swap is R7 conformance, not a move on a cost-versus-quality frontier (spec/DECISIONS.md). Scope and exit criteria rewritten to match: R7 is the first filter and is not traded, the golden gate and the budgets choose among the entries that pass it, and the old 'if evidence does not justify a change, MiniLM remains the default' clause is gone -- under R7-hard it was unreachable as written, since the incumbent's declared language set is English alone (bench/models.json), so MiniLM staying is a disclosed non-conformance rather than a verdict on the evidence
--- body ---
## Scope

After technical validation works, decide which curated entries are exposed and
which one is the default. R7 conformance is the first filter and it is not
traded against anything: an entry whose declared language set is not
multilingual is not a candidate for the default, whatever it scores (the
author's ruling, `spec/DECISIONS.md` 2026-08-31 -- the swap is a conformance
repair, not a move on a cost-versus-quality frontier). The private golden
retrieval gate and the published resource budgets then choose among the entries
that pass that filter. Compatibility crowd counts are supporting operability
evidence only.

## Exit criteria

- Every proposed shipped entry passes local compatibility, the golden retrieval
  gate and resource limits on its supported shapes.
- Licences, artifact sizes and runtime support are disclosed beside the entry.
- The decision and its negative controls are recorded in DECISIONS.md.
- The entry proposed as the default is R7-conformant, read from its declared
  language set rather than from its name or its scores.
- If no entry passes, MiniLM stays in place as the disclosed interim state of a
  known non-conformance -- never as a finding that the evidence did not justify
  a change -- and the ticket names which gate blocked which entry.
